\[
(\sqrt{3} + i)^{99} = 2^{99} \left( \cos \frac{99\pi}{6} + i \sin \frac{99\pi}{6} \right) = 2^{99} \left( \cos \frac{33\pi}{2} + i \sin \frac{33\pi}{2} \right)
\]

\[
(\sqrt{3} - i)^{99} = 2^{99} \left( \cos \frac{33\pi}{2} - i \sin \frac{33\pi}{2} \right)
\]

Since \( \frac{33\pi}{2} = 16\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} \), we have:

\[
\cos \frac{33\pi}{2} = \cos \left( 16\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} \right) = \cos \frac{\pi}{2} = 0 \\
\sin \frac{33\pi}{2} = \sin \left( 16\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} \right) = \sin \frac{\pi}{2} = 1
\]

Thus,

\[
(\sqrt{3} + i)^{99} = 2^{99}i \\
(\sqrt{3} - i)^{99} = -2^{99}i
\]

So,

\[
D^{99} = \begin{pmatrix}
2^{99}i & 0 \\
0 & -2^{99}i
\end{pmatrix}
\]

